**Alternative Reading Explanation**
The standing order states: `"Only order from a supplier whose contract for this product is active and permits ordering."`
The most obvious reading treats this as a **pre-selection filter**: suppliers without valid contracts are excluded from the price comparison pool, so the "lowest-cost" title naturally goes to the cheapest *eligible* supplier. If the cheapest supplier has a bad contract, we fall back to the next cheapest valid one.

An alternative literal reading treats it as a **post-selection veto**: we first identify the absolute lowest-cost supplier(s) capable of fulfilling 100 units, completely ignoring contract status. Only after that selection do we evaluate the contract clause. If the chosen supplier's contract fails the check, the order is forbidden entirely rather than cascading to the next cheapest option. This changes the outcome when the cheapest supplier has an expired/blocked contract but a second-cheapest supplier has a valid one. I have implemented this alternative reading, modeling the contract check as a `forbid` predicate applied after the price/quantity selection.

